        "id": "title",
        "type": "markdown",
        "layout": "full",
        "body": "# Keylane SPDK vs raw io_uring: 500M keys, fixed 2 KiB, 12 workers"
      },
      {
        "id": "summary",
        "type": "markdown",
        "layout": "full",
        "sourceId": "results_csv",
        "body": "## Technical summary\n\nSPDK leads random-read and 1:1 throughput, while pure-write throughput is effectively tied. With nearly identical SPDK read throughput, memtier reports a materially higher p99.99 than Valkey, identifying a client-side deep-tail contribution. All accepted runs retained 500M keys with zero misses or memory rejections."
      },
      {
        "id": "headline_metrics",
        "type": "metric-strip",
        "layout": "full",
        "cardIds": ["card_memtier_read", "card_memtier_mix", "card_write_gap"]
      },
      {"id": "memtier_heading", "type": "markdown", "layout": "full", "body": "## memtier backend comparison"},
      {"id": "memtier_chart", "type": "chart", "layout": "full", "chartId": "chart_memtier_qps"},
      {"id": "valkey_heading", "type": "markdown", "layout": "full", "body": "## Valkey backend comparison"},
      {"id": "valkey_chart", "type": "chart", "layout": "full", "chartId": "chart_valkey_qps"},
      {
        "id": "client_finding",
        "type": "markdown",
        "layout": "full",
        "sourceId": "results_csv",
        "body": "## Client-side read tail\n\nOn SPDK, memtier and Valkey differ by only 0.17% in read throughput, but memtier p99.99 is at least 66.8% above Valkey's conservative upper bound. On raw io_uring the corresponding gap is at least 68.9%."
      },
      {"id": "tail_chart", "type": "chart", "layout": "full", "chartId": "chart_read_tail"},
      {"id": "results_heading", "type": "markdown", "layout": "full", "body": "## Exact accepted results"},
      {"id": "results_table", "type": "table", "layout": "full", "tableId": "table_results"},
      {
        "id": "methodology",
        "type": "markdown",
        "layout": "full",
        "sourceId": "technical_report",
        "body": "## Methodology\n\nKeylane used 12 pinned workers on CPUs 0-11; local user processes and mlx5 IRQs used CPUs 12-15. Both clients used 80 connections, eight threads, pipeline one, unlimited load, and 300-second windows. Defrag remained enabled: read 8/0/0, random 1:1 2/15ms/0, and write 6/0/0. Each client used a separately rebuilt native-key-format dataset because memtier emits unpadded numeric keys while Valkey emits 12-digit zero-padded random placeholders."
      },
      {
        "id": "anomaly",
        "type": "markdown",
        "layout": "full",
        "sourceId": "technical_report",
        "body": "## raw Valkey read anomaly\n\nThe first raw Valkey read contained a 5.4-second throughput collapse at seconds 277-282 and is excluded from the headline table. An immediate 300-second rerun was stable. The original evidence is retained, and no Keylane, kernel, or NVMe SMART error accompanied the event."
      },
      {
        "id": "limitations",
        "type": "markdown",
        "layout": "full",
        "sourceId": "technical_report",
        "body": "## Limitations\n\nThis is a single-host point comparison rather than repeated independent trials. Client-native key formats differ by a few bytes. memtier write followed its mixed workload, while Valkey write followed only read, so cross-client write differences also include different prior aging. raw NVMe managed IRQs remain on worker CPUs and are intentionally part of the io_uring result."
      },
      {
        "id": "recommendations",
        "type": "markdown",
        "layout": "full",
        "sourceId": "technical_report",
        "body": "## Recommended next steps\n\n1. Prefer SPDK when random-read or mixed throughput is the priority.\n2. Prefer raw io_uring when simpler Linux operation outweighs a 5.7-8.2% read penalty; pure-write throughput is tied.\n3. Use Valkey for deep-tail read characterization and memtier for workload-mix generation.\n4. If the raw read disturbance recurs, capture block issue/completion, io_uring completion, per-worker queue delay, and IRQ timing together."
      }
    ]
  },
